## 2026-10-27 evidence identity and direct reprocessing correction

- Confirmed the AA collision: Coding and Intelligence rows share raw data, but the old evidence fingerprint omitted benchmark and metric. The fingerprint now includes both, and persistence removes/rekeys the legacy collision from retained AA raw data. Refresh triggers fitness recomputation after repair, including when another source is incomplete.
- Complete exact misses now require a case-folded exact ID in the active synced provider catalogs before HF or Ollama Search. The existing HF route remains available for catalog matches; no matching name, alias, prefix, or substring is accepted.
- Added an internal reprocessor and explicit bundled operator entry. It enumerates all active synced runtime model IDs with no sample limit, loads one AA/official snapshot, reparses retained AA raw data, materializes exact ID or unique exact-name matches, returns matched/ambiguous/missing/unverified source receipts and write counts, and recomputes global plus effort-profile fitness once. New identity-only rows start nonclaimable, only new rows settle to scored/review-required/unrated outcomes after recompute, and conflicts preserve existing status. It creates no run/job row and cannot claim queued work. The final overlay copies the esbuild bundle into `/app`; it runs only through an explicit `node` invocation with `DATA_DIR=/app/data`.
- RED proof: removing benchmark/metric from the fingerprint made `retained AA raw evidence repairs both metrics before returning its score` fail because only one AA metric row remained (`1 !== 2`). Bypassing the catalog gate made `complete direct miss stays unrated when model ID is absent from active synced catalogs` fail because HF/Search calls reached 2 instead of 0. The retained-AA regression also failed (`3 !== 4`) when the same-ID skip ignored changed raw payload. Restoring the fixes passes these regressions; all tests use mocked fetch, with no live search or inference.
- Retained-AA repair skips only when both `sourceVersion` and raw payload match the current feed. Queue repair reads every retained AA row for that model without scanning other models; the regression puts the legacy row beyond 100 newer records, checks changed-payload and both-metric persistence, then verifies repeated refresh is idempotent.
- Offline results: full `modelBenchmarkSearch.test.ts` passes 44/44; after reducing the regression fixture to the minimum 100 newer rows, the retained-AA test passes 1/1. `modelBenchmarkFitnessSync.test.ts` passes 5/5. Operator coverage includes 140 active IDs, 31 provider-prefixed IDs, one unmatched ID, ambiguous names, incomplete-source receipts, runtime-ID persistence, nonclaimable queue exclusion, and profile publication. The operator bundle builds successfully at 11.5 MB and passes `node --check`; `git diff --check` passes.
- `npm run typecheck:core` exits 2 with 757 repository diagnostics; filtered output contains no diagnostics for changed TypeScript files. Code Simplifier reviewed the final implementation and test changes, including the per-model unbounded reader and the 100-row fixture. Prettier passes on changed TypeScript, scripts, and OpenSpec Markdown.
- Independent Luna-xhigh review found the retained-AA changed-payload/100-row issue; the correction and regression closed it. Follow-up review confirms no remaining code findings across AA repair, direct-only operator, and packaging. The reviewer requested completion of this review record and task 4.4; both are now recorded.
- Strict OpenSpec validation remains pending because the `openspec` CLI is not installed in this worktree; the gate is listed in `openspec/TODO.md`.
- Remaining live work: after integration, disable startup models.dev sync and queue drain, run the bundle across the complete active catalog, confirm source evidence reaches available band candidates, and verify final fitness receipts. After direct evidence is ready, the separately authorized synthetic override cleanup must verify whitelist hash `15f674bc4b8baacb3033a25c5220a842c050e060d2f11193aad297e704cb11d1`, take a private DB backup, and delete only exact key/value/timestamp matches with a concurrent-change guard. No live rows, backup, PR, merge, or deployment were changed in this local package.

## 2026-10-27 direct operator packaging follow-up

- The PR #39 image was healthy, but one explicit direct operator invocation
  failed before database work with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` for
  `@aws-sdk/client-bedrock-runtime`. The affected deployed image was
  `ghcr.io/etcetera-agency/omniroute@sha256:c51c4d12acaaf038eb7634eef62d50ce1b660f3c5b828be35e167550eb7cacd9`.
- The import trace showed the direct operator statically loaded
  `modelBenchmarkSearch.ts`, which statically imported
  `modelBenchmarkSearchProcessor.ts`; that processor imports `handleChat` and
  the executor registry, which loads the Bedrock runtime SDK at module scope.
  Direct evidence reprocessing does not use search-result normalization, so
  this inference-only graph was unnecessary for that entry point.
- Moved the pure normalization model/retry constants and parser into
  `modelBenchmarkSearchNormalization.ts`. The search module now dynamically
  imports `modelBenchmarkSearchProcessor.ts` only from queued search
  normalization. This adds a lazy normalization chunk and removes that
  processor's Bedrock SDK dependency from the operator's eager graph; shared
  search-provider helpers remain eagerly imported.
- RED was reproduced against the prior artifact in the runtime dependency
  tree: the direct graph contained the unresolved Bedrock SDK import. GREEN:
  the new artifact resolver traversed 130 emitted files and resolved 31
  external imports without evaluating modules; the artifact contains no eager
  Bedrock SDK import. The actual bundled operator's `--check-runtime` mode then
  imported its app modules successfully in an isolated Node 24 runtime tree
  with `@aws-sdk/client-bedrock-runtime` absent. The check used only a temporary
  `/tmp/model-benchmark-search-runtime-check` data directory and stopped before
  catalog reads or reprocessing; no network/provider/search/queue calls or
  production database writes occurred.
- The final runtime Docker stage now runs the emitted-graph resolver and the
  actual bundle's import check under `RUN --network=none`, after copying the
  bundle into `/app`. Existing benchmark-search tests pass 44/44, fitness-sync
  tests pass 5/5, and the artifact dependency resolver test passes. RED/GREEN
  tests and the source-level direct-reprocessing regressions remain meaningful.
- Independent release review confirmed `getSearchProvider` and
  `executeWebSearch` remain eager imports of the shared search module, so their
  dependencies are present in the direct bundle. The direct operator does not
  invoke these helpers, and no import-time network call was identified. This is
  a nonblocking dependency-load observation; no further split is scoped absent
  a concrete missing dependency or side effect.
- Code Simplifier reviewed the source change and test; no smaller change
  preserved the direct/queued import boundary. Independent Luna-xhigh review
  found one unused local binding in the resolver test; it was removed, and
  follow-up review found no remaining issue. It also asked that this review
  record include the packaging failure, fix, verification, and release gates;
  that record is now complete.
- This worktree could not run the full final image build: its host is AMD64,
  while the authoritative image is ARM64, and the Dockerfile also requires the
  unavailable `backend_export` build input. The final-stage checks are wired
  into the Docker build, but their execution in that image remains a release
  gate. After integration, build the authoritative ARM64 image and retain the
  smoke receipt. Then follow the maintenance gate before running the operator
  across all active raw catalog IDs. Strict OpenSpec CLI validation also
  remains pending because the CLI is unavailable in this worktree.

## 2026-10-27 raw-ID free-band consumer follow-up

- Traced the active identity contract end-to-end: the active synced catalog supplies raw `model.id`, `virtualFactory` passes it to fitness lookup as `candidate.model`, and the separately provider-prefixed `candidate.modelStr` is used for routing. The free high-tool filters resolve `benchmark_fitness:high` for coding and general before static fallback sources.
- Added an offline regression with active NVIDIA catalog IDs, mocked AA and official source responses, and the direct reprocessor. It verifies scores are persisted under the raw ID for both coding/default categories, both actual free `coding_high_tools` and `general_high_tools` paths admit the higher-scoring target while excluding a lower-scoring free, tool-capable peer, and the returned route ID remains `nvidia/z-ai/glm-5.3`.
- Code Simplifier reviewed the test-only change; an unused fitness-sync import was removed, the candidates use the real active connection ID, and no formula, precedence, or range changes were needed.
- Verification: `tests/unit/autoCombo/bands-quality-e2e.test.ts` passes 5/5 with the current worktree's `vitest.mcp.config.ts` and root, including the new regression; `git diff --check` passes. The existing native config-loader warning about `__dirname` remains.
- Independent Luna-xhigh review initially found that target-only inclusion checks would not prove the quality filter excluded other candidates. The regression now checks direct high-profile scores for both models, verifies the peer scores lower while remaining free/tool-capable, and requires each actual band output to contain only the target. Follow-up review found no remaining actionable findings.

## 2026-10-27 Docker build-context correction

- The final runtime stage copied its resolver smoke from `tests/unit/build`, but root `.dockerignore` excludes `tests`. Added ordered exact-path exceptions that keep all other test content excluded. A minimal BuildKit build used this repository as the actual root context with `FROM scratch` and `COPY tests`; it exported only the resolver smoke, which matched the tracked file byte-for-byte. This proves context inclusion only; the authoritative ARM64 image build and final-stage smoke remain release gates.

## 2026-10-27 ARM64 resolver-location correction

- Main image run `40504168756` passed the resolver-test `COPY` then failed at test line 46: Node `v26.10.0` resolved `undici` from the test module's `/tmp` location, not `/app/node_modules`. `require.resolve('undici')` from `/app` returned `/app/node_modules/undici/index.js`. Dockerfile now copies the smoke to `/app/scripts/ops` so normal module resolution uses the image's runtime dependencies; missing packages still fail the gate.
- On the ARM64 server, an isolated container using immutable runtime image `sha256:c51c4d12acaaf038eb7634eef62d50ce1b660f3c5b828be35e167550eb7cacd9` reproduced the `/tmp` failure, then passed the same resolver test under `/app` (130 emitted files, 31 external imports) and actual bundled `--check-runtime` (`runtime module graph loaded`). Container used `--network=none`, read-only root/artifacts, tmpfs `/tmp`, and no production data, env, or key mounts. The operator import smoke applied migrations only to the tmpfs database. Failed run stopped before image publish or deploy; production container was not touched.
- The next automatic main ARM64 build/runtime gate remains required before deployment and the live maintenance acceptance.
